/*****************************************************************************
    familyT -- THE GROUND CHECK for the argument-list family (Clay's relay,
    "table arc opening moves", 2026-08-01).

    THE HYPOTHESIS UNDER TEST, as briefed: the seven list-taking ops ARE the
    two-pointer write-back family, and if that is exact then per-element
    iterate-and-fire is fold-left BY CONSTRUCTION -- each call mutates target,
    the next call sees it, and order is preserved by emission order.

    THIS FIXTURE ANSWERS THE HALF THAT SOURCE-READING CANNOT: the ORDER. Every
    one of the six iterating ops walks with `argument.prior(...)`, which is a
    BACKWARD list walk. Whether that yields SOURCE order depends entirely on
    how aCTionExpressioN builds the expression list -- a fact about the builder,
    not about the walker. Reading `prior` and concluding "reversed" is exactly
    the causal-claim shape CLAUDE.md says fails here; reading it and MEASURING
    is the structural one that holds.

    ⚠ WHY `+%` AND NOT `-=` OR `/=`, and this is the rung-design note applied
    rather than quoted. The brief asks for a case where per-element and one-shot
    COULD differ -- a happy set proves nothing. But `-=` and `/=` OVER A LIST
    ARE ORDER-INDEPENDENT ARITHMETIC: 100-1-2-3 is 94 in any order, and 1000/2/5
    is 1000/5/2. They are non-commutative as BINARY ops and order-BLIND as
    FOLDS, so they cannot discriminate and would have gone green on a reversed
    walk. `+%` is order-INJECTIVE: three distinct tags land in a list whose
    read-back order IS the fold order, so `P Q R` and `R Q P` are different
    answers, not a lucky match.

    ⚠⚠ MEASURED RESULT, 2026-08-01, and THE FAMILY IS NOT UNIFORM. The brief
    asked for a ground check rather than an assertion; this is why. Three
    different shapes among the seven:

      ORDER (row 1)          `+%` folds in SOURCE order -- P Q R, not R Q P.
                             So `argument.prior(...)` walking "backward" over
                             the list yields source order, because that is how
                             aCTionExpressioN builds it. FOLD-LEFT CONFIRMED,
                             and it could only be confirmed by running.

      `+=` DOES NOT ITERATE  (row 3, and row 2's numE). The op the probe is
                             aimed at is the one member with no per-element
                             arm at all: its isLIST arms are whole-list -- the
                             35a string-concat arm (one appendGroup call, which
                             does its own traversal) and the copyListTo bulk
                             arm. "One glance, any of the seven" would have
                             reported the family uniform, because six of seven
                             do iterate and `+=` is the seventh.

      `-=` AND `*=` ARE      (row 2) -- a PRE-EXISTING defect, not something
      SILENTLY WRONG         this work introduced:
                                 100 -= 1 2 3  -> 100   SILENT NO-OP  (want 94)
                                 100 *= 2 3    -> 0     SILENT WRONG  (want 600)
                                 100 /= 2 5    -> 10    correct
                                 100 -= 7      -> 93    scalar, correct
                             That much is MEASURED and it is the part to carry.

    ⚠ THE MECHANISM IS **NOT** ESTABLISHED, AND A FIRST ATTEMPT AT ONE WAS
    WITHDRAWN THE SAME HOUR. The obvious story reads straight off the source:
    `-=` and `*=` test their scalar arm as `data && argument.data` without
    checking the argument is a SCALAR, so a list node would satisfy
    `argument.data`, the switch would run with the list node as operand
    (`argument.count` == 0), and the `or argument.isLIST` per-element arm below
    would be unreachable. It fits both numbers exactly, and `/=` -- the one that
    is correct -- is also the one that guards on the ARGUMENT's type
    (`argument.isCOUNT || argument.isNUMBER`). Tidy, and probably still the
    answer.

    BUT `incant/tableProbe` went looking for its load-bearing premise and found
    the opposite: **a node carrying a list reports `datA = 0`**, i.e. NO data --
    both a define-block group (`dataNames`) and one built with `+%` (`listy`).
    If an expression-list node behaves the same way, `argument.data` is FALSE
    and the arm cannot be the one that ran. Two ways out, neither measured: an
    aCTionExpressioN-built list node may differ from a define-built one, or the
    zero may come from somewhere else entirely.

    SO IT IS FILED AS: behaviour measured, cause OPEN. This is CLAUDE.md's
    measured asymmetry doing exactly what it says -- the structural claim (three
    of seven disagree) held; the causal claim (which arm ran) did not survive
    its own check. Confirming it needs instrumentation on the expression-list
    node, not more source reading.

    ⚠ THIS IS THE ARGUMENT FOR THE PROMOTION, not against it. Three of seven
    members disagree about a property the family is supposed to share, and the
    disagreement is invisible at every call site. A dispatch tree GENERATED from
    a table cannot have two members order their arms differently by hand -- the
    defect class stops being possible rather than being fixed.

    NOT FIXED HERE. Reported, not repaired: `-=`/`*=` arm ordering is Tony's
    call, it is pre-existing, and the probe's whole point is to decide whether
    these get repaired by hand or deleted by generation.

    SENTINEL AT THE FOOT (CLAUDE.md third corollary): an incant parse failure
    abandons the rest of the file and still exits 0.
*****************************************************************************/
Start();
include(unitTests);
include(utilities);

search reset stack Grokking UnitTests Utilities list;

